> Does ADVFs keep that index in memory?

No.  It is a file on disk.  You can look at it using the 

        /usr/sbin/showfile -i directory

command.  The -i option displays the information about the directory's 
index file.  man showfile

AdvFS does cache recently accessed pages, so the directory and the index 
file would most likely be in cache for a 2nd pass.

But I would not expect the index file to be used for a /bin/ls -f or 
even a /bin/ls operation.  The index file would only get called into 
play if the ls command was doing lstat() operations on the files, like 
when you do an 'ls -l' or any other ls option that needs to get file 
specific information.  Directories only hold the name and tag of the 
file (where tag can be equated to a UFS inode number).  All other 
information about a file is kept in metadata files.

The reason the index file would not get called into play for a simple ls 
is that the only thing being done is to sequentially read the directory 
to get the names and tags of the files.  The index file is not needed 
for that.

The index file comes into play when doing a random lookup of a file, or 
adding a new file to make sure it does not already exist and to find an 
empty spot in the directory to stick the new name as well as updating 
the directory and the index with the new name, and finally if you are 
removing a name from the directory, the lookup to find the name and the 
work needed to remove the name from the directory and the index.

If the ls is told to ask for more than just the name of the files in the 
directory, then the ls command must lstat() the names, and the lstat() 
operation will cause a random lookup in the directory and that is when 
the index file gets called into play during an ls.

If there is sufficient memory on the system to cache all the file 
metadata after doing something like an 'ls -l' then the first one will 
be a bear because of all the random disk seeks to find all the metadata, 
but the 2nd one should go faster if it can find all the metadata sitting 
in the cache.  But you have to have a lot of idle memory sitting around 
to hold the metadata for 500,000 files.  Plus on v5.1 the AdvFS cache is 
fixed in size as a percent of physical memory.  I think the default was 
that AdvFS was allowed to use up to 30% of physical memory (or was it 7% 
- it has been too long and I forget things too easily :-).  It was a 
tunable.  In some later version, AdvFS was integrated with the UBC, so 
that it could  all of memory if VM did not want it, or use just a little 
of memory if VM's demands were stronger.

