> 200,000 files to delete, so I guess it would have been faster anyway.

I think that /bin/ls -1r would just change the name sorting order, but I 
do not think it would cause an lstat() operation.  And since you have 
index files, there is not as much benefit to removing the files in a 
specific order.  But if you want to squeeze out very last drop of 
performance, then I think 

        /bin/ls -1f directory | xargs rm -f

would be about as good as it gets.  You might squeeze a few tiny drops 
out of writing your own C program to read the directory using readdir() 
and call remove().  That would eliminate multiple invocations of the rm 
command, but the xargs command does bunch up the files so that it does 
not need to invoke rm too many times.  I think ls -f into xargs is good 
enough.

> regards,
> Jeremy Thomson

I don't know what the files in the huge directory(s) are used for, if 
you have a way to pruning them on a regular basis, say via a cron job, 
it might help with the sudden load demand to do this before the vdump.

                                        Bob Harris


